{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED: the checkpoint repair at exact head efd228ebface98b2a833793f9fbe209f5962610b, against request 673267bd7da89145ed4cd3f6e6d70f57eab0dc24 and promise da3624864aaecf153e4dd9e97aef687c2686ccc9. Worktree clean at that head; make vet, make test (12 packages), make race, make build and git diff --check all pass.\n\nAll three findings are closed, and closed by tests that name their own subject rather than catching them in passing. The combined payload-and-attachment ceiling — the half that was green last time while its payload-alone sibling was pinned — now fails TestCheckpointPayloadAndAttachmentsShareOneCeiling, and the sibling still fails its own test, so the two halves are independently held. The configured size cap fails TestCheckpointMarshalEnforcesDocumentedSizeLimit; moving it into marshalCheckpoint with an explicit limit is better than what I asked for, since the bound is now a parameter a test can drive rather than a package constant a test has to work around. And the cadence-gated failure path is pinned: I made a failed write advance checkpointAttempt on the gated branch, which is precisely the postponement my last report said nothing detected, and TestReaderRetriesCheckpointWriteAfterFailure fails.\n\nI re-ran the coverage pass as well as the mutations, because that was the number that mattered and a repair could add tests without moving it. Never-executed error returns went from twenty of forty-three to **seven of forty-four**. The seven that remain are almost all error-wrapping paths — descriptor, sequence, frontier, payload — reached only when an underlying call fails, plus two bare ErrNoUsableCheckpoint returns. That is a different class from the guards that were missing before.\n\nThe one I flagged in my promise is also resolved. Last round the payload-tree identity check was caught by a ceiling-named test, so I could not tell whether the identity half had a test of its own. Mutating it now fails TestCheckpointMetadataBindsEnvelopeTrailersAndTreeIndependently — a binding test, for its own reason. I spot-checked two more of the boundaries you listed: trailing JSON fails TestCheckpointDecoderRejectsTrailingJSONAtItsBoundary, and the begins-the-named-sequence guard fails TestCheckpointSequencePositionGuards.\n\nTwo disclosures about my own run, both of which would have been false findings.\n\nI first recorded a race-detector failure at this head. It was mine: I ran `go test -race ./...` from the repository root, and in this pre-promotion layout the module lives under spike/, so it failed setup rather than finding a race. `make race`, which does the `cd spike` the Makefile intends, passes. I checked the log before reporting rather than trusting my own exit code, which is the only reason it did not become a blocker in this report.\n\nAnd one mutation was inert: I changed the checkpoint-marker error *string* rather than its condition, got green, and briefly had it down as an untested guard. Changing a message tests nothing unless something asserts the message. I re-ran against conditions instead. That is the third inert-mutation trap I have walked into across these reviews, and it is always the same shape — a mutation that compiles and runs but does not alter behaviour looks exactly like a guard with no teeth.\n\nThe basis handling is right too: the artifact names this exact head without traversing the stale prior report, which is what let the exact-head guard accept it.","body":{"branch":"task/resident-checkpoint","head":"efd228ebface98b2a833793f9fbe209f5962610b","verdict":"APPROVED"}}